TI  - Contracture of the infraspinatus muscle in a Cavalier King Charles Spaniel
AU  - Bergmann, H
AU  - Forterre, F
AU  - Gaschen, L
AU  - Bornand, V
AU  - Schawalder, R
T2  - WIENER TIERARZTLICHE MONATSSCHRIFT
AB  - Introduction
Contraction of the canine infraspinatus muscle was first reported in literature in 1970. Hunting and working breeds are most commonly affected. To our knowledge no previous report describes this pathology in small breed dogs.
Case Report
A 6.5-year-old Cavalier King Charles Spaniel was presented to the Small Animal Clinic of the Vetsuisse Faculty, University of Bern, for examination of a left forelimb lameness of 6 weeks duration. Abduction of the left forelimb while standing, atrophy of the infraspinatus, supraspinatus and deltoid muscles, pain with palpation and flexion of the shoulder joint and a lameness with outwards rotation of the shoulder were the abnormal clinical findings. Contracture of the infraspinatus muscle caused the lameness, and tenectomy of the infraspinatus tendon resulted in restoration of a normal gait.
Conclusion
Contraction of the infraspinatus muscle is a pathologic entity that has to be considered as a differential diagnosis in small breed dogs with shoulder pain.
